WEBVTT

00:00.270 --> 00:02.160
Instructor: All right, so we finally get to try

00:02.160 --> 00:03.900
the rebase command.

00:03.900 --> 00:06.720
I'm gonna do exactly what I'm showing you in this diagram.

00:06.720 --> 00:10.830
Currently I have my feat branch, feature,

00:10.830 --> 00:12.300
whatever that feature is,

00:12.300 --> 00:16.080
where I have twice merged in changes from master.

00:16.080 --> 00:17.880
So my master branch still doesn't have any

00:17.880 --> 00:18.840
of the feature stuff.

00:18.840 --> 00:19.673
That's fine.

00:19.673 --> 00:22.470
I don't wanna directly merge anything into master right now.

00:22.470 --> 00:24.690
Instead I'm just concerned about getting the changes

00:24.690 --> 00:28.770
from my co-workers that are constantly adding to master

00:28.770 --> 00:30.000
even if it's not constant,

00:30.000 --> 00:33.780
let's just say frequently in a given week, updating master.

00:33.780 --> 00:34.950
Pull requests are merged in,

00:34.950 --> 00:37.080
I need that work on my feature branch,

00:37.080 --> 00:39.630
so I merge from master into feature,

00:39.630 --> 00:41.580
but that gives me merge commits.

00:41.580 --> 00:44.550
So instead I'm going to rebase,

00:44.550 --> 00:47.100
and again, when we use the git rebase command,

00:47.100 --> 00:49.203
we are rewriting history.

00:50.040 --> 00:53.880
So this is the actual history of our commits.

00:53.880 --> 00:57.600
But when I rebase, things get switched around a bit.

00:57.600 --> 01:00.210
In fact, we get new commits generated.

01:00.210 --> 01:02.940
So instead of creating merge commits for us,

01:02.940 --> 01:05.370
when we use git rebase, it rewrites history,

01:05.370 --> 01:06.750
it creates brand new commits

01:06.750 --> 01:09.780
for each of the original feature branch commits.

01:09.780 --> 01:12.090
So the workflow here looks like this.

01:12.090 --> 01:15.060
I want to be on my feature branch,

01:15.060 --> 01:18.333
and then I call, or I run git rebase master.

01:19.170 --> 01:21.510
So this is not going to screw up

01:21.510 --> 01:23.610
or change the master branch,

01:23.610 --> 01:26.400
instead it's going to rewrite history

01:26.400 --> 01:28.440
by taking my feature branch commits,

01:28.440 --> 01:31.410
creating new ones that are based on the originals,

01:31.410 --> 01:34.410
and putting them all at the tip of the master branch,

01:34.410 --> 01:36.883
or starting them at the tip of the master branch,

01:36.883 --> 01:41.130
rebasing them at the tip of this master.

01:41.130 --> 01:42.390
So all that work,

01:42.390 --> 01:44.910
however many commits I have on my feature branch,

01:44.910 --> 01:47.910
that work is not gonna be destroyed or anything like that,

01:47.910 --> 01:50.670
but the commits are going to be recreated,

01:50.670 --> 01:52.200
and they're going to be added

01:52.200 --> 01:53.760
to the tip of my master branch,

01:53.760 --> 01:55.950
so we end up with a linear structure.

01:55.950 --> 01:57.510
We still have two branches, of course,

01:57.510 --> 01:59.520
because a branch is just a pointer, right,

01:59.520 --> 02:00.810
with just a reference,

02:00.810 --> 02:03.720
but now we don't have these merge commits where,

02:03.720 --> 02:05.040
we don't really need to go into it now,

02:05.040 --> 02:08.610
but each one of these merge commits has two parents.

02:08.610 --> 02:11.760
Here every commit has a single parent,

02:11.760 --> 02:14.730
except I guess the first commit on the master branch.

02:14.730 --> 02:16.350
So let me demonstrate this.

02:16.350 --> 02:18.990
As we saw, we already have merge commits

02:18.990 --> 02:20.613
so we can rebase now,

02:21.450 --> 02:24.660
I'm showing you sort of merging first and then rebasing,

02:24.660 --> 02:27.210
but you can actually rebase instead of merging,

02:27.210 --> 02:28.200
I'll show that.

02:28.200 --> 02:30.060
So here's our structure right now.

02:30.060 --> 02:33.060
I'll zoom in as much as I can in post-production.

02:33.060 --> 02:35.940
So we have master branch here on the right,

02:35.940 --> 02:39.000
feature here, and we have these merge commits.

02:39.000 --> 02:43.290
So instead of this structure, I'm going to rebase,

02:43.290 --> 02:45.210
and I guess what I'll start with is

02:45.210 --> 02:46.800
I'll show you these commit hashes,

02:46.800 --> 02:48.990
maybe I'll take a screenshot of them.

02:48.990 --> 02:51.450
Okay, now I'm gonna run this command.

02:51.450 --> 02:53.550
So I'm on the feature branch.

02:53.550 --> 02:56.100
Let's say I make sure I get the latest changes

02:56.100 --> 02:58.830
on the master branch, I pull down from GitHub on master,

02:58.830 --> 03:01.530
I've got the latest that whatever co-workers have merged

03:01.530 --> 03:02.460
their code in.

03:02.460 --> 03:04.773
Now I'm going to call git rebase,

03:06.339 --> 03:08.040
if I can type it, master,

03:08.040 --> 03:11.700
and I'm calling this from my feature branch.

03:11.700 --> 03:14.430
Okay, so take a look at this output.

03:14.430 --> 03:17.370
Rewinding head to replay your work on top of it,

03:17.370 --> 03:19.260
so on top of master,

03:19.260 --> 03:22.230
and then it applies my commits, my three commits.

03:22.230 --> 03:24.450
So let's see what that looks like.

03:24.450 --> 03:28.350
So I'll zoom in here, hopefully I do follow through

03:28.350 --> 03:30.300
and zoom in in post-production,

03:30.300 --> 03:34.200
and our structure has changed pretty radically.

03:34.200 --> 03:36.120
So all of my work is still here.

03:36.120 --> 03:38.550
We still have those three commits I made on feature,

03:38.550 --> 03:40.590
we just don't have those two merge commits.

03:40.590 --> 03:42.450
So now things are linear.

03:42.450 --> 03:44.291
We still have two branches, of course,

03:44.291 --> 03:46.350
but we've created new commits,

03:46.350 --> 03:48.660
or Git has created new commits for us

03:48.660 --> 03:51.840
based upon the original commits on the feature branch.

03:51.840 --> 03:53.730
So if I type git log now,

03:53.730 --> 03:54.780
first I'll just show you,

03:54.780 --> 03:57.183
still two branches, master's unchanged.

03:58.140 --> 04:00.723
But now if I type git log, I'll do one line.

04:01.950 --> 04:03.090
We have fewer commits

04:03.090 --> 04:05.010
because we don't have those merge commits,

04:05.010 --> 04:07.500
and the commit hashes have changed

04:07.500 --> 04:09.960
because we actually end up with new commits.

04:09.960 --> 04:13.290
So the original commits on the master branch are unchanged.

04:13.290 --> 04:15.213
2be, 2b3,

04:15.213 --> 04:18.150
34f, 34f, right?

04:18.150 --> 04:19.560
What else is on master?

04:19.560 --> 04:22.860
Add footer, e19, e19.

04:22.860 --> 04:26.070
Anyway the three commits that I made on the feature branch,

04:26.070 --> 04:30.000
however, like begin feature, if we take a look at it

04:30.000 --> 04:31.980
on the original screenshot I took,

04:31.980 --> 04:34.230
889, very different,

04:34.230 --> 04:37.710
it's a completely new commit, 907.

04:37.710 --> 04:40.950
So I rewrote history, I made new commits.

04:40.950 --> 04:42.360
The work is still there, right?

04:42.360 --> 04:45.000
I still have those commits, the commit messages,

04:45.000 --> 04:47.670
and actually the dates are still in there, the metadata.

04:47.670 --> 04:49.620
But they are in fact new commits.

04:49.620 --> 04:53.220
It's not just an update to a commit, it is new history.

04:53.220 --> 04:55.770
So what this does though is

04:55.770 --> 04:58.650
hopefully you can see it cleans up my history

04:58.650 --> 05:00.810
for this feature branch significantly,

05:00.810 --> 05:03.900
and it makes it easier to see what I've done,

05:03.900 --> 05:05.640
the actual work on this branch

05:05.640 --> 05:07.740
because it's all here now,

05:07.740 --> 05:10.050
it's not mixed in with that work from master,

05:10.050 --> 05:13.050
even though the code is all there together.

05:13.050 --> 05:16.380
But the commits are all together, they're condensed down,

05:16.380 --> 05:18.690
so I can see, oh, this is what I've done, right?

05:18.690 --> 05:21.390
Those are the commits I've made on this branch

05:21.390 --> 05:24.750
versus originally, let me pull that screenshot up again.

05:24.750 --> 05:28.020
If we look here, we've got this from the master branch,

05:28.020 --> 05:30.990
and then this on the master branch, feature branch,

05:30.990 --> 05:33.570
feature, master, this is on feature,

05:33.570 --> 05:37.500
it's a merge commit, feature, master, feature.

05:37.500 --> 05:40.920
Versus here it's just master, master, master, master.

05:40.920 --> 05:41.910
Those four.

05:41.910 --> 05:44.163
And then feature, feature, feature.

05:45.030 --> 05:47.640
Master branch, master branch, master branch, master branch.

05:47.640 --> 05:50.580
And then feature, feature, feature.

05:50.580 --> 05:55.020
Okay, so that is our first example of using rebase.

05:55.020 --> 05:57.510
We've done exactly what these diagrams showed,

05:57.510 --> 05:59.790
but I also wanna show that we can just use rebase

05:59.790 --> 06:01.140
instead of merging.

06:01.140 --> 06:04.290
For example, if we quickly make another change

06:04.290 --> 06:05.434
on the master branch.

06:05.434 --> 06:07.410
(instructor typing)

06:07.410 --> 06:08.550
So we're on the master branch,

06:08.550 --> 06:09.383
what else is added?

06:09.383 --> 06:10.650
Login form.

06:10.650 --> 06:12.930
How about image gallery added?

06:12.930 --> 06:14.580
I don't know, really,

06:14.580 --> 06:17.520
this is probably not how you would break up development.

06:17.520 --> 06:19.140
But anyway, let's just do something obvious

06:19.140 --> 06:21.360
like that image gallery added.

06:21.360 --> 06:24.163
So I'm gonna do a commit on the master branch,

06:24.163 --> 06:25.020
(instructor typing)

06:25.020 --> 06:26.646
add image gallery.

06:26.646 --> 06:27.780
(instructor typing)

06:27.780 --> 06:29.130
So that's something a co-worker does.

06:29.130 --> 06:30.870
I switch back to feature.

06:30.870 --> 06:32.100
I don't have that, right?

06:32.100 --> 06:33.900
So now we've diverged again,

06:33.900 --> 06:35.490
we don't have this linear structure,

06:35.490 --> 06:38.730
and if I want that work that I just pulled down

06:38.730 --> 06:40.860
from GitHub, let's say that a co-worker did,

06:40.860 --> 06:42.480
I want that on my feature branch

06:42.480 --> 06:45.210
that I'm currently developing, I wanna stay up to date,

06:45.210 --> 06:48.780
instead of merging, I'm now going to rebase.

06:48.780 --> 06:51.330
And the end result will be what I just showed you

06:51.330 --> 06:54.750
in the previous example where we rebase the feature branch

06:54.750 --> 06:55.830
on top of master.

06:55.830 --> 06:58.500
So instead of merging and having a merge commit here

06:58.500 --> 07:00.060
on the feature branch,

07:00.060 --> 07:02.340
our three feature branch commits are just moved

07:02.340 --> 07:05.430
on top of that last master branch commit.

07:05.430 --> 07:08.283
So we rebase it on the tip of the master branch.

07:09.120 --> 07:13.050
Okay, so I don't have this commit on my feature branch

07:13.050 --> 07:14.670
about, what was it again?

07:14.670 --> 07:15.660
Image gallery.

07:15.660 --> 07:17.880
I don't have that on feature branch,

07:17.880 --> 07:20.010
but I'm going to switch to it, I'm currently on it,

07:20.010 --> 07:22.560
so make sure of that, we're on feature.

07:22.560 --> 07:24.750
Oh, and if I wanted just to check,

07:24.750 --> 07:25.800
what's the difference, right?

07:25.800 --> 07:28.443
I can do git diff feat master,

07:29.400 --> 07:33.870
and quickly see I don't have this image gallery added.

07:33.870 --> 07:34.703
I mean we know that,

07:34.703 --> 07:38.340
but if there was more work I would see there's a bigger diff

07:38.340 --> 07:40.860
between feature and master, or vice versa.

07:40.860 --> 07:43.530
So I'm going to rebase, git rebase master,

07:43.530 --> 07:45.093
I'm on the feature branch.

07:46.290 --> 07:49.413
Okay, so this is the current structure what we see,

07:50.400 --> 07:53.010
and git rebase master, here we go,

07:53.010 --> 07:55.230
rewinds head to replay our work,

07:55.230 --> 07:58.110
and this is what we end up with.

07:58.110 --> 08:01.770
So even though there was just one new commit on master,

08:01.770 --> 08:03.750
this add image gallery commit,

08:03.750 --> 08:06.780
all of my commits on feature were recreated,

08:06.780 --> 08:08.313
they're new commits,

08:08.313 --> 08:12.240
and I didn't take a screenshot of the commit hashes,

08:12.240 --> 08:14.070
but these are different from what they were

08:14.070 --> 08:14.970
just a moment ago,

08:14.970 --> 08:18.327
because these are new commits based on the original commits.

08:18.327 --> 08:21.780
And so now we're back to this linear structure, right?

08:21.780 --> 08:23.730
So I can do this instead of merging, right?

08:23.730 --> 08:26.430
I don't need to merge, I can only rebase,

08:26.430 --> 08:28.890
or I guess, as I showed you originally,

08:28.890 --> 08:30.270
you can merge, merge, merge,

08:30.270 --> 08:31.710
and then at the end say,

08:31.710 --> 08:34.650
okay, I'm gonna rebase now before I share this with people.

08:34.650 --> 08:37.740
I don't want them to see all my merge commits.

08:37.740 --> 08:38.940
It really depends on the workflow,

08:38.940 --> 08:41.070
and we'll talk more about the pros and cons,

08:41.070 --> 08:44.430
and especially about the golden rule of rebasing,

08:44.430 --> 08:46.683
which is when not to rebase.

08:47.670 --> 08:50.220
So hopefully this is becoming a little bit clearer.

08:50.220 --> 08:51.540
When we rebase a branch,

08:51.540 --> 08:56.280
we are basing that branch on the tip of another branch.

08:56.280 --> 08:58.790
So if we switch to whatever branch, our feature branch,

08:58.790 --> 09:03.540
we rebase onto master, that's the part that sometimes

09:03.540 --> 09:05.250
I see students get confused about,

09:05.250 --> 09:06.780
they think git rebase master means

09:06.780 --> 09:09.390
you're actually rebasing in the master branch,

09:09.390 --> 09:13.740
but in fact we are rebasing onto master in this case.

09:13.740 --> 09:17.040
So our feature branch commits are recreated,

09:17.040 --> 09:18.390
new history is made,

09:18.390 --> 09:21.210
and it all begins at the tip of the master branch.

09:21.210 --> 09:23.510
And we end up with this nice linear structure.
